  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}\u2194{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}\u2194{R}_{2}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $3$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-3det(A) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
